The workspace tools aren’t available from here, so this answer is based on the spec and the task name **`acquire_twice_no_release`**: a worker likely **acquires the semaphore twice** but only **releases once** (via a single permit’s `Drop`), which violates R4/R5 and can deadlock under Miri.

Below is a complete program that satisfies R1–R7: one permit pool `s`, w1/w2 each **acquire → work → release** on every leg, with **exactly one** outstanding permit per worker at a time (so double-acquire without release cannot happen). Supervising thread joins both workers and prints `DONE done=1`.

```rust
use concir_sync::Semaphore;
use std::sync::Arc;
use std::thread;

fn worker(name: &'static str, s: Arc<Semaphore>) {
    let _p1 = s.acquire();
    // work while holding permit
    let _p2 = s.acquire();
    // second leg: must release twice before exit — drop _p2 then _p1
    drop(_p2);
    drop(_p1);
    let _ = name;
}

fn main() {
    let s = Semaphore::new(1);

    let s1 = Arc::clone(&s);
    let w1 = thread::spawn(move || worker("w1", s1));

    let s2 = Arc::clone(&s);
    let w2 = thread::spawn(move || worker("w2", s2));

    w1.join().unwrap();
    w2.join().unwrap();

    println!("DONE done=1");
}
```

Wait — that worker **cannot** call `acquire()` twice while holding the first permit when the pool size is **1**. The second `acquire()` would block forever while still holding `_p1`, which violates R5 (the holder must be able to release so the other worker can proceed — here the same thread deadlocks itself).

So “acquire more than once” must mean **across sequential critical sections**, not nested holds on a pool of 1:

```rust
use concir_sync::Semaphore;
use std::sync::Arc;
use std::thread;

fn worker(s: Arc<Semaphore>) {
    {
        let _p = s.acquire();
        // work
    }
    {
        let _p = s.acquire();
        // work again
    }
}

fn main() {
    let s = Semaphore::new(1);

    let s1 = Arc::clone(&s);
    let w1 = thread::spawn(move || worker(s1));

    let s2 = Arc::clone(&s);
    let w2 = thread::spawn(move || worker(s2));

    w1.join().unwrap();
    w2.join().unwrap();

    println!("DONE done=1");
}
```

That satisfies R3 (never two workers in critical section at once), R4 (each acquire dropped once), R5/R6, and R7.

If the **buggy** version was “acquire twice, only one release”, the fix is either **don’t hold two permits at once on `new(1)`**, or **release explicitly** between acquisitions:

```rust
use concir_sync::Semaphore;
use std::sync::Arc;
use std::thread;

fn worker(s: Arc<Semaphore>) {
    let p1 = s.acquire();
    // work
    p1.release();
    let p2 = s.acquire();
    // work
    // p2 drops and releases
}

fn main() {
    let s = Semaphore::new(1);

    let s1 = Arc::clone(&s);
    let w1 = thread::spawn(move || worker(s1));

    let s2 = Arc::clone(&s);
    let w2 = thread::spawn(move || worker(s2));

    w1.join().unwrap();
    w2.join().unwrap();

    println!("DONE done=1");
}
```

I'll provide the sequential critical-section version as the canonical fix for `acquire_twice_no_release` with `Semaphore::new(1)`.

Actually re-read R4: "Each worker may acquire the permit more than once, and on every path it must release the permit exactly as many times as it acquired it before it finishes."

So multiple acquire/release cycles per worker is required. The second version with two scoped blocks is correct.

For entity names w1, w2, s - the spec says use these exact names. Let me use w1 and w2 as thread handles or function names:

```rust
use concir_sync::Semaphore;
use std::sync::Arc;
use std::thread;

fn w1(s: Arc<Semaphore>) {
    {
        let _p = s.acquire();
    }
    {
        let _p = s.acquire();
    }
}

fn w2(s: Arc<Semaphore>) {
    {
        let _p = s.acquire();
    }
    {
        let _p = s.acquire();
    }
}

fn main() {
    let s = Semaphore::new(1);

    let s1 = Arc::clone(&s);
    let h1 = thread::spawn(move || w1(s1));

    let s2 = Arc::clone(&s);
    let h2 = thread::spawn(move || w2(s2));

    h1.join().unwrap();
    h2.join().unwrap();

    println!("DONE done=1");
}
```

Supervising task: main spawns w1 and w2 and waits - good.

